> EphemeralToken[\"Ephemeral Scoped MCP Token (kb_eat_*)\"]\n    EphemeralToken --> BoundaryCheck[\"CredentialBoundary Bitmask Gate\"]\n    BoundaryCheck --> RLSContext[\"PostgreSQL Session Context (SET LOCAL app.credential_boundary)\"]\n    RLSContext --> ForceRLS[\"PostgreSQL FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY Filter\"]\n```\n\n![Workload OIDC Federation Studio](images/slice3-workload-oidc-studio.png)\n\n---\n\n## 2. Role-Bound HKDF-HMAC Internal Service Authentication\n\nTicket `KAN-63` establishes hardened inter-service authentication (`kanban/security/service_auth.py`) governing direct communication between background runners, sync daemons, and application servers.\n\n### HKDF Key Derivation\n\nSubkeys are derived on demand from the system master key encryption key (`master_kek`) using HMAC-based Extract-and-Expand Key Derivation Function (HKDF-SHA256, RFC 5869):\n\n$$\\text{Subkey} = \\text{HKDF-Expand}(\\text{Extract}(\\text{master\\_kek}, \\text{salt}), \\text{\"internal-service-v2/\"} \\parallel \\text{principal}, 32)$$\n\nThis derivation ensures:\n- Cryptographic isolation between service roles (`runner`, `relay`, `auditor`, `migrator`).\n- A compromise of an individual service subkey does not compromise the root `master_kek` or other subsystem keys.\n\n### Canonical Request Envelope Signing\n\nWhen a service invokes an internal API endpoint, it packages and signs a canonical request envelope:\n\n$$\\text{Signature} = \\text{HMAC-SHA256}\\left(\\text{Subkey}, \\text{Timestamp} \\parallel \\text{Principal} \\parallel \\text{Method} \\parallel \\text{Path} \\parallel \\text{SHA256}(\\text{Body})\\right)$$\n\nThe receiving endpoint validates the signature according to strict security invariants:\n- **Timestamp Skew Limit:** Requests with timestamps drifting more than 300 seconds from the server clock are immediately rejected (`CLOCK_DRIFT_EXCEEDED`), neutralizing replay attacks.\n- **Constant-Time Verification:** Signatures are compared using constant-time string comparison (`hmac.compare_digest`), eliminating timing side-channel attacks.\n- **Header Tampering Defense:** The canonical envelope binds the HTTP method, URI path, and SHA-256 digest of the request body, preventing reverse-proxy header injection or request modification in flight.\n\n### ASGI TLS Scope Client Certificate Extraction\n\nFor mutual TLS (mTLS) client validation, \ud83d\udc4dkanban never trusts raw HTTP headers such as `X-SSL-Client-Cert` or `X-KB-Service-Principal`, which can be injected by untrusted intermediaries. Instead, the service authenticator extracts certificate attributes directly from the ASGI connection scope:\n\n```python\n# Verifiable TLS scope extraction in kanban/security/service_auth.py\nclient_cert = scope.get(\"client_cert\")\nif not client_cert:\n    raise ServiceAuthError(\"Missing verified mTLS client certificate in ASGI scope\")\n\ncert = x509.load_der_x509_certificate(client_cert)\ncommon_name = cert.subject.get_attributes_for_oid(x509.NameOID.COMMON_NAME)[0].value\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Headless Workload Identity Federation (RFC 8693)\n\nTicket `KAN-74` implements headless Workload Identity Federation and RFC 8693 OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange, eliminating the need to store long-lived static API secrets or passwords inside CI/CD repositories.\n\n### The RFC 8693 Token Exchange Flow\n\nExternal automated workloads (e.g. GitHub Actions runners, GitLab CI jobs, Kubernetes pods) obtain short-lived, permission-scoped tokens using their native OpenID Connect (OIDC) identity tokens:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant CI as GitHub Actions / GitLab CI Runner\n    participant IdP as Identity Provider (GitHub / GitLab OIDC)\n    participant KB as \ud83d\udc4dkanban Token Endpoint (/oauth/token)\n    participant DB as PostgreSQL (workload_identity_policies)\n\n    CI->>IdP: Request OIDC ID token (aud: https://kanban.example.com)\n    IdP-->>CI: Signed JWT ID token\n    CI->>KB: POST /oauth/token (grant_type=token-exchange, subject_token=JWT)\n    KB->>IdP: Fetch discovery document & JWKS public keys\n    KB->>KB: Verify cryptographic signature (RS256, ES256, EdDSA)\n    KB->>DB: Query matching policy for issuer and subject pattern\n    KB->>DB: Atomically insert JTI into workload_jti_replays\n    DB-->>KB: Insert successful (no conflict)\n    KB-->>CI: Short-lived scoped token (kb_eat_*) with CredentialBoundary\n```\n\n### Security & Invariant Rules\n\n1. **Strict Cryptographic Algorithm Allowlist:** Only asymmetric signature algorithms (`RS256`, `PS256`, `ES256`, `EdDSA`) are permitted. Symmetric algorithms (`HS256`) and unsigned tokens (`alg=\"none\"`) are rejected with `400 invalid_grant`.\n2. **SSRF-Hardened OpenID Discovery:** Provider discovery and JWKS retrieval reuse the secure discovery client, enforcing private IP address blocking (RFC 1918 / RFC 4193), timeout caps, and response size limits.\n3. **Atomic Replay Prevention:** Each OIDC JWT carries a unique token identifier (`jti`). The server executes an atomic PostgreSQL insert into `workload_jti_replays` (`ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`). If the same `jti` is submitted a second time, the transaction aborts with `401 WORKLOAD_TOKEN_REPLAYED`.\n4. **Subject Pattern & Claim Filtering:** Workload identity policies define glob patterns for the token subject (`sub`), such as `repo:org/repo:ref:refs/heads/main`, alongside mandatory JSON claim subsets (e.g. `{\"environment\": \"production\"}`).\n5. **Token Lifetime Cap:** Workload identity tokens enforce an explicit lifetime ceiling (`max_ttl_seconds <= 86400`, default 3,600 seconds / 1 hour), mitigating credential exposure windows.\n\n### Workload Identity Policy Specification\n\nPolicies are managed by administrators via `/api/v1/admin/workload-identities` (requires AAL2):\n\n| Attribute | Type & Validation | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | String (1 to 120 chars) | Descriptive policy label displayed in admin lists |\n| `description` | String or `None` | Optional markdown description of workload context |\n| `issuer` | Strict HTTPS URL (1 to 500 chars) | OIDC identity provider issuer URL |\n| `client_id` | String (1 to 255 chars) | Expected audience (`aud`) claim in OIDC token |\n| `subject_pattern` | Glob or Regex (1 to 500 chars) | Matching pattern for token `sub` claim |\n| `required_claims_json` | JSON Object | Exact claims required in the OIDC JWT payload |\n| `surface` | `agent` or `docs` | Target MCP surface granted to the federated token |\n| `scopes` | List of Strings | Allowed OAuth scopes (e.g. `[\"agent\", \"read:issues\"]`) |\n| `allowed_project_ids` | List of UUIDs or `None` | Restricts token access to specific project IDs |\n| `capability_mask` | Integer (`0` to `255`, default `255`) | Monotonic capability ceiling enforced by RLS |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | List of CIDR Strings or `None` | Restricts token exchange to specific IP subnets |\n| `max_ttl_seconds` | Integer (`60` to `86400`, default `3600`) | Maximum lifespan of issued ephemeral token |\n| `is_enabled` | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | Toggles active status of the workload policy |\n\n---\n\n## 4. CredentialBoundary Enforcement & RLS Isolation\n\nTokens issued to automated agents, external workloads, and third-party integrations are governed by the `CredentialBoundary` capability bitmask (tickets `KAN-66` and `KAN-67`).\n\n### Monotonic Capability Bitmasks\n\nThe `CredentialBoundary` bitmask restricts the maximum capabilities a token can exercise, regardless of the assigned user role:\n\n| Capability Bit | Bitmask Value | Description & Bound |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `DENY_ALL` | `0x0000` (`0`) | Zero privilege; all operations rejected with `INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY` |\n| `READ_ISSUES` | `0x0001` (`1`) | Read work items and comments within project scope |\n| `WRITE_ISSUES` | `0x0002` (`2`) | Create and modify issues and work items |\n| `TRANSITION_ISSUES`| `0x0004` (`4`) | Execute workflow state transitions |\n| `READ_DOCS` | `0x0008` (`8`) | Access technical documentation and project stickies |\n| `WRITE_DOCS` | `0x0010` (`16`) | Author and edit documents |\n| `EXECUTE_AGENT` | `0x0020` (`32`) | Invoke autonomous agent helper routines |\n| `ADMIN_READ` | `0x0040` (`64`) | Read non-destructive system diagnostics |\n| `FULL_ADMIN` | `0x00FF` (`255`) | Complete administrative privileges |\n\nWhen an incoming request is authenticated:\n1. The middleware computes the effective capability set: $\\text{Effective} = \\text{UserRoleCapabilities} \\cap \\text{CredentialBoundary}$.\n2. The middleware sets PostgreSQL session Grand Unified Configuration (GUC) variables inside the transaction:\n   ```sql\n   SET LOCAL app.current_principal_id = 'usr_workload_agent';\n   SET LOCAL app.credential_boundary = '0x0007';\n   ```\n3. PostgreSQL Row-Level Security policies evaluate `app.credential_boundary` before returning or mutating records. If the capability bit is missing, PostgreSQL returns zero records or aborts the write with a constraint violation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Implementation Recipes\n\n### Recipe 1: GitHub Actions CI/CD Configuration\n\nTo authenticate a GitHub Actions workflow to the \ud83d\udc4dkanban Model Context Protocol (MCP) server without storing permanent secrets:\n\n```yaml\nname: Automated Agent CI/CD Integration\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\npermissions:\n  id-token: write\n  contents: read\n\njobs:\n  kanban-sync:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - name: Checkout Code\n        uses: actions/checkout@v4\n\n      - name: Authenticate to \ud83d\udc4dkanban via RFC 8693\n        id: auth\n        run: |\n          # 1. Fetch OIDC ID token from GitHub Actions environment\n          ID_TOKEN=$(curl -sLS \\\n            -H \"Authorization: bearer ${ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_TOKEN}\" \\\n            \"${ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL}&audience=https://kanban.example.com\" | jq -r .value)\n\n          # 2. Exchange OIDC token for short-lived scoped MCP token\n          RESPONSE=$(curl -sLS -X POST \"https://kanban.example.com/oauth/token\" \\\n            -H \"Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\" \\\n            -d \"grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\" \\\n            -d \"subject_token=${ID_TOKEN}\" \\\n            -d \"subject_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt\" \\\n            -d \"audience=https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent\")\n\n          TOKEN=$(echo \"${RESPONSE}\" | jq -r .access_token)\n          echo \"::add-mask::${TOKEN}\"\n          echo \"mcp_token=${TOKEN}\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\"\n\n      - name: Execute Model Context Protocol Tool\n        run: |\n          curl -sLS -X POST \"https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent/tools/call\" \\\n            -H \"Authorization: Bearer ${{ steps.auth.outputs.mcp_token }}\" \\\n            -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n            -d '{\n              \"name\": \"read_ticket\",\n              \"arguments\": {\"identifier\": \"KAN-42\", \"lod\": \"summary\"}\n            }'\n```\n\n---\n\n### Recipe 2: GitLab CI/CD Configuration\n\n```yaml\nkanban-automation:\n  stage: test\n  id_tokens:\n    GITLAB_OIDC_TOKEN:\n      aud: https://kanban.example.com\n  script:\n    - |\n      RESPONSE=$(curl -sLS -X POST \"https://kanban.example.com/oauth/token\" \\\n        -H \"Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\" \\\n        -d \"grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\" \\\n        -d \"subject_token=${GITLAB_OIDC_TOKEN}\" \\\n        -d \"subject_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt\" \\\n        -d \"audience=https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent\")\n      TOKEN=$(echo \"${RESPONSE}\" | jq -r .access_token)\n      curl -sLS -X POST \"https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent/tools/call\" \\\n        -H \"Authorization: Bearer ${TOKEN}\" \\\n        -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" \\\n        -d '{\"name\": \"search_tickets\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"CI Pipeline\", \"lod\": \"summary\"}}'\n```\n\n---\n\n### Recipe 3: Kubernetes Projected Service Account Token Configuration\n\n```bash\n# Obtain projected service account token from Kubernetes pod volume\nK8S_TOKEN=$(cat /var/run/secrets/tokens/kanban-audience)\n\n# Exchange projected token with \ud83d\udc4dkanban\nRESPONSE=$(curl -sLS -X POST \"https://kanban.example.com/oauth/token\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\" \\\n  -d \"grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:token-exchange\" \\\n  -d \"subject_token=${K8S_TOKEN}\" \\\n  -d \"subject_token_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:token-type:jwt\" \\\n  -d \"audience=https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent\")\n\nACCESS_TOKEN=$(echo \"${RESPONSE}\" | jq -r .access_token)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Visual Reference and Studio Screenshots\n\n![Workload OIDC Federation Studio](images/slice3-workload-oidc-studio.png)\n\n![MCP Access Studio Key Minting](images/slice3-mcp-access-studio-mint-modal.png)\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Bulk Purge](images/slice3-oauth-governance-bulk-purge.png)\n\n![OAuth and Workload Governance Studio](images/42-admin-oauth-governance.png)\n"
