++-    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 11 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspaces`) manages top-level tenancy, membership defaults, data lifecycle policies, and security guardrails.\n\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable name of the workspace displayed in header and navigation | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at provisioning | Public within workspace; visible to all members and invited guests |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace identifier used in API routes and web paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 64 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable after creation; binds database RLS tenancy context |\n| `default_role` | Default permission tier assigned to newly provisioned local users | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER`, `WORKSPACE_VIEWER`, `WORKSPACE_GUEST` | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER` | Restricts baseline capabilities before explicit project assignments |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Number of days soft-deleted items remain in Trash Studio before automated purge | Integer between 7 and 365 | 30 | Governs compliance data retention; automated purge requires daemon runner |\n| `auto_archive_threshold_days` | Inactivity duration after which closed tickets are automatically archived | Integer between 0 and 730 (0 disables auto-archive) | 90 | Keeps active board queries lightweight; archived tickets remain search-indexable |\n| `max_projects_limit` | Maximum number of active projects allowed within this workspace | Positive integer (1 to 1000) | 50 | Resource cap preventing tenant sprawl and memory exhaustion |\n| `air_gap_enforced` | Strict zero-egress flag blocking all external cloud AI and runner connections | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When enabled, only local Ollama models and internal runners execute |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Comma-separated domain allowlist for user registration and invite cards | List of valid domain strings (e.g. `corp.example.com`) | Empty (Allow all verified) | Rejects non-corporate email addresses at registration gate |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project-level attributes, work item defaults, view presets, and planning dimensions.\n\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Display name of the project | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in command palette and breadcrumbs |\n| `key` | Prefix key for issue identifiers (e.g. `KAN-142`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 characters) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates human-readable issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `description` | Markdown overview and charter for the project | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown (up to 10,000 characters) | Empty | Sanitized server-side; safe Mermaid and media proxying enabled |\n| `default_workflow_id` | Identifier of active workflow managing state transitions | Valid UUID of existing project workflow | Default Kanban Workflow | Determines active transition gates, validators, and AI agent checks |\n| `auto_archive_days` | Project-specific threshold for archiving closed work items | Integer (0 to 365, 0 = inherit workspace) | 0 | Overrides workspace retention for high-velocity software teams |\n| `enable_cycles` | Toggles Agile/Scrum cycle and sprint planning features | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Enables cycle velocity tracking, start/end dates, and rollover |\n| `enable_modules` | Toggles functional module breakdown and progress roll-ups | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Allows hierarchical grouping of work items under functional deliverables |\n| `wip_limits_enforcement` | Behavior when Kanban column WIP limits are exceeded | `warn`, `strict_block`, `disabled` | `warn` | `strict_block` rejects card drop operations exceeding column capacity |\n| `default_assignee_mode` | Initial assignment policy for newly authored work items | `unassigned`, `project_lead`, `creator` | `unassigned` | Prevents orphaned tickets and automates triage assignment |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) defines state groups, transition helper hooks, synchronous validators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `state_name` | Human-readable label for the workflow state | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required (e.g. `In Review`) | Appears on board columns, peek drawers, and transition selectors |\n| `state_group` | Standardized category mapping for cycle metrics | `backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled` | `started` | Used in velocity burn-down and sub-issue progress roll-ups |\n| `helper_type` | Engine used to evaluate transition eligibility | `wasm_validator`, `wasm_mutator`, `agent_gate` | `wasm_validator` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `hook_path` | Target module, wasm bytecode, or agent identifier | File path, slug, or registered agent UUID | Required | Executes within sandboxed environment with strict resource caps |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if transition helper rejects or times out | `BLOCK_TRANSITION`, `WARN_AND_ALLOW`, `PROVISIONAL_HOLD` | `BLOCK_TRANSITION` | Fail-closed invariant preventing invalid transitions into protected states |\n| `execution_timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed wall-clock execution time | 50ms to 30000ms (Sync Wasm: <=250ms) | 100ms | Prevents hanging transitions and thread pool starvation |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Isolation level enforced during helper execution | `wasm` (in-process wasmtime) or `nsjail` (container) | `wasm` | Sandboxed with zero network or filesystem syscall access |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) defines dynamic metadata, computed story points, and sandboxed formulas.\n\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable label displayed on ticket rails and tables | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed alongside execution status badge (`\ud83e\uddea`) |\n| `slug` | Unique field identifier used in formulas and JSON payloads | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and computation engine | `scalar`, `formula`, `wasm`, `nsjail` | `formula` | Determines performance budget and standalone mode availability |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `scalar`, `wasmtime` (Tier A), `nsjail` (Tier B) | `wasmtime` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Token identity provided during script execution | `INVOKING_USER`, `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | `INVOKING_USER` | `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` requires dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `PROJECT`, `WORKSPACE`, `SERVER` | `PROJECT` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, `json_table` | `text` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen output format | JSON object matching schema specification | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or container script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Comma-separated domain list (e.g. `api.github.com`) | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) configures enterprise identity providers, WebAuthn Passkeys, LDAP trees, and break-glass recovery.\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_type` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP`, `WEBAUTHN`, `TOTP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake and token exchange |\n| `card_enabled` | Master active toggle for the authentication card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled cards reject authentication attempts immediately |\n| `sort_rank` | Order in which login cards appear on `/login` screen | Non-negative integer (lower ranks appear first) | 10 | Allows organizations to prioritize SSO while preserving local admin cards |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | String (Always canonical `\ud83d\udc4dkanban`) | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or spoofing |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint URL | Valid HTTPS URL | None | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth/OIDC application client identifier | ASCII string | None | Public identifier registered with enterprise identity provider |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth/OIDC client secret credential | Encrypted string (write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never exposed over REST API |\n| `ldap_host` | LDAPS directory hostname or IP address | Hostname or IP | None | LDAPS on port 636 or StartTLS required; plaintext LDAP rejected |\n| `ldap_bind_dn` | Distinguished Name used for directory lookup | LDAP DN string (e.g. `cn=admin,dc=corp,dc=com`) | None | Service account with read-only search permissions across user tree |\n| `break_glass_active` | Emergency lockout prevention bypass trigger | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Allows emergency local admin access when SSO is offline |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `key_name` | Descriptive name of the API token or client tool | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `token_prefix` | Standardized key prefix identifying privilege level | `kb_agt_*`, `kb_doc_*`, `kb_adm_*` | Auto-assigned | Cryptographically identifies target surface before database lookup |\n| `lod_tier` | Default Level of Detail returned in tool responses | `summary`, `standard`, `full` | `standard` | Controls response verbosity; `summary` minimizes agent context consumption |\n| `rate_limit_rpm` | Maximum allowed tool invocations per minute | Integer between 10 and 600 | 120 | Protects database connection pool from runaway autonomous agent loops |\n| `expires_at` | Expiration timestamp for the API key | ISO-8601 Timestamp or `None` | 90 days from creation | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED` |\n| `destructive_gate` | Enables two-stage confirmation for destructive operations | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Requires short-lived confirmation token before permanent deletions |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (1=View, 3=Comment, 31=Member, 255=Admin) | 255 (Full) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 1 restricts scopes to `${surface}:read` |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, and configures token lifetimes.\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `kb_client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforced` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `S256` | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `read:issues`, `write:issues` | `agent` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (300 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai`) registers model providers, configures write-only API keys, and sets monthly token consumption caps.\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_key` | Underlying AI provider implementation | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `custom` | `gemini` | Selects prompt serializer, tool calling dialect, and streaming pipeline |\n| `priority` | Fallback dispatch order when multiple providers are active | Integer (1 to 100, higher runs first) | 10 | Primary provider attempted first; falls back on rate limit or 5xx |\n| `api_endpoint` | Base URL for API communications | Valid HTTP/HTTPS URL | Official cloud endpoint | Local Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Cryptographic API secret for provider access | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never sent back to browser client |\n| `monthly_token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget ceiling | Non-negative integer (0 = unlimited) | 1,000,000 | Blocks cloud invocations once budget is reached to prevent cost overruns |\n| `default_model` | Model identifier used for sparkle and transition gates | String (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`) | Provider default | Balances reasoning capability against response latency |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model generations | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 0.2 | Low temperatures ensure deterministic responses for code and QA gates |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`) authors system personas, binds autonomous agents to service accounts, and configures execution harnesses.\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `agent_name` | Unique name and identifier for the autonomous agent | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Used in ticket mentions, activity streams, and assignment cards |\n| `system_prompt` | Core instructions, persona guidelines, and security boundaries | UTF-8 Markdown text | Required | Directs model reasoning; stripped of dangerous injection sequences |\n| `execution_harness` | Runtime environment executing agent invocations | `api_direct`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `api_direct` | `cli_pipe` uses zero-shell `agy -p` or `codex -p` in Linux jail |\n| `cli_pipe_binary` | Binary path used for zero-shell CLI pipe harness | Pre-approved binary name (`agy`, `codex`) | `agy` | Executes without shell or bash; communicates strictly over STDIN/STDOUT |\n| `service_principal_id` | Bound service account identity for database RLS | Valid UUID of service principal | Auto-provisioned | Binds database permissions to caller identity via `SET LOCAL` |\n| `repository_access` | Git repository access mode granted to the agent | `none`, `ro_mount`, `ephemeral_clone` | `none` | `ro_mount` mounts repository strictly read-only for forensic QA |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) configures distributed build nodes, mints enrollment tokens, and monitors mTLS heartbeats over dedicated port `:8444`.\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `runner_name` | Unique hostname or identifier of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health dashboard and active job telemetry |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic token for runner registration | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires after 15 minutes (900 seconds max); grants certificate signing request (CSR) |\n| `mtls_cert_fingerprint`| SHA-256 fingerprint of validated client certificate | Hexadecimal SHA-256 hash | Registered upon enroll | Hardware-bound identity verifying mutual TLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Standard enrollment invocation command | Shell command string | Generated on mint | Syntax: `echo \"$TOKEN\" \\| uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `max_concurrent_jobs` | Maximum simultaneous task executions allowed on runner | Integer between 1 and 16 | 2 | Throttles runner workload to prevent resource exhaustion |\n| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | Maximum allowable duration without ping before offline | Integer between 10 and 120 seconds | 30 | Dead runners are marked offline and active jobs rescheduled |\n| `allowed_repo_remotes` | Whitelist of Git repository URLs runner can clone | Comma-separated list of repository URLs | Empty (All authorized) | Prevents runner exploitation for arbitrary network exfiltration |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `STANDALONE_CONTAINER`, `MULTI_CONTAINER_COMPOSE` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `backup_encryption_passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Used by PBKDF2 to encrypt standalone archive into KBBAK1 format |\n| `target_postgres_url` | External database URL used during promotion cutover | Valid PostgreSQL connection string | None | Must support UTF-8, RLS, and required extensions (`ltree`, `pgvector`) |\n| `target_redis_url` | External Redis connection string for WebSocket pub/sub | Valid Redis URL (`redis://` or `rediss://`) | None | Decouples distributed realtime events from single-process memory |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (default `/var/backups/kanban`) | `/var/backups/kanban` | Requires write permissions; accessible for disaster recovery download |\n| `auto_promotion_verify` | Runs comprehensive post-migration verification suite | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Asserts object link integrity and user access manifests before cutover |\n"
+++    "content": "# [Informational] Studio Configuration Reference\n\nAuthoritative technical reference specification for all configuration attributes, execution tiers, and administrative controls across all 14 \ud83d\udc4dkanban Studios.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Workspace Studio\nThe Workspace Studio (`/workspaces/:ws/settings` or `/admin/workspaces`) manages top-level tenancy, membership defaults, data lifecycle policies, and security guardrails.\n\n\n### Workspace Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable name of the workspace displayed in header and navigation | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at provisioning | Public within workspace; visible to all members and invited guests |\n| `slug` | URL-safe workspace identifier used in API routes and web paths | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens (2 to 64 chars) | Derived from name | Immutable after creation; binds database RLS tenancy context |\n| `default_role` | Default permission tier assigned to newly provisioned local users | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER`, `WORKSPACE_VIEWER`, `WORKSPACE_GUEST` | `WORKSPACE_MEMBER` | Restricts baseline capabilities before explicit project assignments |\n| `trash_retention_days` | Number of days soft-deleted items remain in Trash Studio before automated purge | Integer between 7 and 365 | 30 | Governs compliance data retention; automated purge requires daemon runner |\n| `auto_archive_threshold_days` | Inactivity duration after which closed tickets are automatically archived | Integer between 0 and 730 (0 disables auto-archive) | 90 | Keeps active board queries lightweight; archived tickets remain search-indexable |\n| `max_projects_limit` | Maximum number of active projects allowed within this workspace | Positive integer (1 to 1000) | 50 | Resource cap preventing tenant sprawl and memory exhaustion |\n| `air_gap_enforced` | Strict zero-egress flag blocking all external cloud AI and runner connections | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | When enabled, only local Ollama models and internal runners execute |\n| `allowed_email_domains` | Comma-separated domain allowlist for user registration and invite cards | List of valid domain strings (e.g. `corp.example.com`) | Empty (Allow all verified) | Rejects non-corporate email addresses at registration gate |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project Studio\nThe Project Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings`) configures project-level attributes, work item defaults, view presets, and planning dimensions.\n\n\n### Project Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Display name of the project | UTF-8 String (1 to 128 characters) | Required at creation | Scoped to workspace; surfaced in command palette and breadcrumbs |\n| `key` | Prefix key for issue identifiers (e.g. `KAN-142`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 characters) | Required at creation | Immutable; generates human-readable issue keys across all surfaces |\n| `description` | Markdown overview and charter for the project | Canonical UTF-8 Markdown (up to 10,000 characters) | Empty | Sanitized server-side; safe Mermaid and media proxying enabled |\n| `default_workflow_id` | Identifier of active workflow managing state transitions | Valid UUID of existing project workflow | Default Kanban Workflow | Determines active transition gates, validators, and AI agent checks |\n| `auto_archive_days` | Project-specific threshold for archiving closed work items | Integer (0 to 365, 0 = inherit workspace) | 0 | Overrides workspace retention for high-velocity software teams |\n| `enable_cycles` | Toggles Agile/Scrum cycle and sprint planning features | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Enables cycle velocity tracking, start/end dates, and rollover |\n| `enable_modules` | Toggles functional module breakdown and progress roll-ups | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Allows hierarchical grouping of work items under functional deliverables |\n| `wip_limits_enforcement` | Behavior when Kanban column WIP limits are exceeded | `warn`, `strict_block`, `disabled` | `warn` | `strict_block` rejects card drop operations exceeding column capacity |\n| `default_assignee_mode` | Initial assignment policy for newly authored work items | `unassigned`, `project_lead`, `creator` | `unassigned` | Prevents orphaned tickets and automates triage assignment |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Workflow Transition Studio\nThe Workflow Transition Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/workflows`) defines state groups, transition helper hooks, synchronous validators, and asynchronous AI agent gates.\n\n\n### Workflow Transition Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `state_name` | Human-readable label for the workflow state | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required (e.g. `In Review`) | Appears on board columns, peek drawers, and transition selectors |\n| `state_group` | Standardized category mapping for cycle metrics | `backlog`, `unstarted`, `started`, `completed`, `cancelled` | `started` | Used in velocity burn-down and sub-issue progress roll-ups |\n| `helper_type` | Engine used to evaluate transition eligibility | `wasm_validator`, `wasm_mutator`, `agent_gate` | `wasm_validator` | Governs execution latency and synchronous vs asynchronous processing |\n| `hook_path` | Target module, wasm bytecode, or agent identifier | File path, slug, or registered agent UUID | Required | Executes within sandboxed environment with strict resource caps |\n| `failure_policy` | Action taken if transition helper rejects or times out | `BLOCK_TRANSITION`, `WARN_AND_ALLOW`, `PROVISIONAL_HOLD` | `BLOCK_TRANSITION` | Fail-closed invariant preventing invalid transitions into protected states |\n| `execution_timeout_ms` | Maximum allowed wall-clock execution time | 50ms to 30000ms (Sync Wasm: <=250ms) | 100ms | Prevents hanging transitions and thread pool starvation |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Isolation level enforced during helper execution | `wasm` (in-process wasmtime) or `nsjail` (container) | `wasm` | Sandboxed with zero network or filesystem syscall access |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Custom Fields Studio\nThe Custom Fields Studio (`/projects/:proj/settings/custom-fields`) defines dynamic metadata, computed story points, and sandboxed formulas.\n\n\n### Custom Fields Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Human-readable label displayed on ticket rails and tables | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed alongside execution status badge (`\ud83e\uddea`) |\n| `slug` | Unique field identifier used in formulas and JSON payloads | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Derived from name | Used in formula expressions and API JSON properties |\n| `flavor` | Execution tier and computation engine | `scalar`, `formula`, `wasm`, `nsjail` | `formula` | Determines performance budget and standalone mode availability |\n| `sandbox_tier` | Multi-tier sandbox engine used for execution | `scalar`, `wasmtime` (Tier A), `nsjail` (Tier B) | `wasmtime` | `nsjail` requires multi-container compose; fail-closed in standalone |\n| `credential_mode` | Token identity provided during script execution | `INVOKING_USER`, `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | `INVOKING_USER` | `SERVICE_ACCOUNT` requires dual-control cryptographic approval |\n| `target_scope` | Maximum tenant visibility boundary of the field | `PROJECT`, `WORKSPACE`, `SERVER` | `PROJECT` | Escalation beyond `PROJECT` requires AAL2 administrative step-up |\n| `output_format` | Canonical schema used to format and render output | `text`, `number`, `badge`, `severity_pill`, `url`, `markdown_snippet`, `json_table` | `text` | Enforces strict Pydantic validation before persisting to JSONB |\n| `format_metadata` | Configuration options tailored to chosen output format | JSON object matching schema specification | `{}` | Governs decimal precision, color tokens, allowlists, or table headers |\n| `script_source` | Raw source code for formula or script evaluation | Python AST expression, Rust Wasm, or container script | Required | Signed with SHA-256 digest upon commit; immutable against database tampering |\n| `egress_allowlist` | Allowed destination hostnames for nsjail tier | Comma-separated domain list (e.g. `api.github.com`) | Empty (Zero egress) | Network disabled by default via Linux kernel `CLONE_NEWNET` |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Authentication Studio\nThe Multi-Card Authentication Studio (`/admin/auth`) configures enterprise identity providers, WebAuthn Passkeys, LDAP trees, and break-glass recovery.\n\n\n### Authentication Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_type` | Underlying identity protocol card | `LOCAL_ADMIN`, `OAUTH2`, `OIDC`, `LDAP`, `WEBAUTHN`, `TOTP` | Required | Determines credential validation handshake and token exchange |\n| `card_enabled` | Master active toggle for the authentication card | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled cards reject authentication attempts immediately |\n| `sort_rank` | Order in which login cards appear on `/login` screen | Non-negative integer (lower ranks appear first) | 10 | Allows organizations to prioritize SSO while preserving local admin cards |\n| `rp_name` | WebAuthn Relying Party display name | String (Always canonical `\ud83d\udc4dkanban`) | `\ud83d\udc4dkanban` | Displayed by browser and hardware security keys during passkey prompts |\n| `rp_id` | WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier | Strict ASCII FQDN without protocol or port | Hostname of deployment | Must match browser origin to prevent credential theft or spoofing |\n| `issuer_url` | OIDC discovery endpoint URL | Valid HTTPS URL | None | Fetches `/.well-known/openid-configuration` and cryptographic keys |\n| `client_id` | OAuth/OIDC application client identifier | ASCII string | None | Public identifier registered with enterprise identity provider |\n| `client_secret` | OAuth/OIDC client secret credential | Encrypted string (write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never exposed over REST API |\n| `ldap_host` | LDAPS directory hostname or IP address | Hostname or IP | None | LDAPS on port 636 or StartTLS required; plaintext LDAP rejected |\n| `ldap_bind_dn` | Distinguished Name used for directory lookup | LDAP DN string (e.g. `cn=admin,dc=corp,dc=com`) | None | Service account with read-only search permissions across user tree |\n| `break_glass_active` | Emergency lockout prevention bypass trigger | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Allows emergency local admin access when SSO is offline |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MCP Access Studio\nThe MCP Access Studio (`/settings/mcp` or `/admin/mcp`) manages Model Context Protocol API tokens, Level of Detail (LOD) defaults, and rate limits.\n\n![MCP Access Studio and Key Minting Modal](images/slice3-mcp-access-studio-mint-modal.png)\n\n\n### MCP Access Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `surface_type` | Target isolated streamable endpoint | `/mcp/agent`, `/mcp/docs`, `/mcp/admin` | `/mcp/agent` | Determines tool catalog and token budget ceiling (<= 1,850 tokens for agent) |\n| `key_name` | Descriptive name of the API token or client tool | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Surfaced in audit logs and active session inspector |\n| `token_prefix` | Standardized key prefix identifying privilege level | `kb_agt_*`, `kb_doc_*`, `kb_adm_*` | Auto-assigned | Cryptographically identifies target surface before database lookup |\n| `lod_tier` | Default Level of Detail returned in tool responses | `summary`, `standard`, `full` | `standard` | Controls response verbosity; `summary` minimizes agent context consumption |\n| `rate_limit_rpm` | Maximum allowed tool invocations per minute | Integer between 10 and 600 | 120 | Protects database connection pool from runaway autonomous agent loops |\n| `expires_at` | Expiration timestamp for the API key | ISO-8601 Timestamp or `None` | 90 days from creation | Expired keys reject calls with `401 UNAUTHORIZED` |\n| `destructive_gate` | Enables two-stage confirmation for destructive operations | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Requires short-lived confirmation token before permanent deletions |\n| `capability_mask` | Operational capability bitmask ceiling | Integer bitmask (0 to 255, 0=Deny-All, 1=Read-Only, 31=Standard, 255=Admin) | 255 (Full) | Monotonic `CredentialBoundary` ceiling; mask 0 rejects all tools with `INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY` |\n| `allowed_cidrs` | Subnet IP allowlist for incoming MCP invocations | Comma or whitespace separated IPv4/IPv6 CIDRs | None (Allow all) | Rejects calls originating outside specified subnets with `403 FORBIDDEN_CIDR` |\n| `scope_tier` | Tenancy authorization scope boundary | `project`, `workspace`, `global`, `personal` | `project` | Restricts object graph access to specified tenant boundary |\n| `role` | Identity persona bound to minted key | `personal`, `service_bot` | `personal` | Service bot keys enforce strict service account RLS boundaries |\n\n![Connect OAuth App Modal](images/slice3-connect-oauth-modal.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`surface_type`:** Selects the target streamable MCP endpoint. `/mcp/agent` exposes 10 execution and QA tools with a strict token ceiling of <= 1,850 tokens. `/mcp/docs` exposes 3 knowledge and documentation tools capped at <= 1,350 tokens. `/mcp/admin` exposes 12 governance and lifecycle tools capped at <= 2,800 tokens, requiring AAL2 administrative credentials.\n- **`key_name`:** Human-readable label assigned to the key during creation (1 to 64 characters). Surfaced in audit log feeds, active session inspectors, and the visual key management table to identify the calling entity or developer machine.\n- **`token_prefix`:** Standardized 7-character prefix assigned automatically based on surface type and privilege. Keys bound to `/mcp/agent` use `kb_agt_*`, documentation keys use `kb_doc_*`, and administrative keys use `kb_adm_*`. Allows fast static routing and early denial before database lookup.\n- **`lod_tier`:** Default Level of Detail returned in search and read responses. `summary` returns bare identifiers and titles (~40 tokens), `standard` provides descriptions and assignees (~200 tokens), and `full` returns complete object graphs, comment feeds, and activity manifests.\n- **`rate_limit_rpm`:** Configurable per-key sliding-window rate limit (10 to 600 requests per minute, default 120). Protects PostgreSQL connection pools and worker queues from runaway autonomous agent recursion loops. Exceeding callers receive `429 TOO_MANY_REQUESTS`.\n- **`expires_at`:** Key expiration timestamp. The UI offers presets for 30 days, 60 days, 90 days (default), 1 year, and `Unlimited`. Selecting `Unlimited` triggers an explicit security risk warning checkbox that must be acknowledged before the key can be minted. Expired keys are rejected with `401 UNAUTHORIZED`.\n- **`destructive_gate`:** Master safety switch governing dangerous mutations. When enabled on `/mcp/admin`, irreversible deletions (`destructive_lifecycle`) require a two-stage confirmation protocol: requesting a blast-radius summary and receiving a short-lived cryptographic confirmation token, then explicitly confirming execution with that token.\n- **`capability_mask`:** Monotonic 8-bit integer capability bitmask ceiling (0 to 255). A value of `0` creates a deny-all key where all tool calls are rejected with `isError: true` (`INSUFFICIENT_CAPABILITY`). A value of `1` restricts scopes to read-only (`${surface}:read`), `31` grants standard member permissions, and `255` enables full administrative execution. Enforced via PostgreSQL `SET LOCAL app.credential_boundary`.\n- **`allowed_cidrs`:** Optional network boundary allowlist containing comma- or whitespace-separated IPv4 or IPv6 subnets (e.g. `127.0.0.1/32`, `192.168.1.0/24`). Requests originating outside allowed CIDR blocks are rejected immediately with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN_CIDR`.\n- **`scope_tier`:** Binds the minted token to a specific tenant boundary: `personal` (bound to current user), `project` (scoped to a single project key like `KAN`), `workspace` (scoped to all projects in workspace), or `global` (cross-workspace server scope). Project-scoped keys cannot query or mutate tickets in adjacent projects.\n- **`role`:** Selects between `personal` (executing on behalf of an authenticated human user) and `service_bot` (executing as an autonomous background bot). Service bot keys execute under dedicated service accounts with explicit RLS principal contexts.\n\n![MCP Config Snippet and Client Onboarding Modal](images/slice3-mcp-config-snippet-modal.png)\n\n### Connect OAuth App and Client Snippets Modals\n\n- **Connect OAuth App Modal:** Provides one-click visual integration guides and connection snippets for external AI tools:\n  - **Gemini App:** Generates direct OAuth Resource URLs for Google Gemini Connected Apps (`gemini.google.com -> Settings -> Connected Apps -> Add Custom MCP Server`).\n  - **Claude Code & Desktop:** Emits standard `claude_desktop_config.json` stanzas and `claude mcp add` CLI commands.\n  - **VS Code & Cursor:** Emits `mcpServers` configuration blocks with automatic SSE endpoint binding.\n  - **Antigravity CLI:** Emits `agy mcp add kanban <url> --auth oauth` commands.\n  - **OpenAI Codex:** Emits `codex mcp add kanban <url> --auth oauth` commands.\n- **MCP Config Snippet Modal:** Generates copy-paste configuration snippets for newly minted static API keys across Claude Code, Cursor, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, and OpenAI Codex. Includes a write-only custody warning reminding operators that secrets will never be displayed again.\n\n![Workload OIDC Federation Studio](images/slice3-workload-oidc-studio.png)\n\n### Workload Identity Federation Studio (`/admin/workload-identities`)\n\nThe Workload Identity Federation Studio allows administrators to author and manage RFC 8693 token exchange policies for headless CI/CD runners:\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `name` | Descriptive policy identifier | String (1 to 120 characters) | Required | Displayed in workload policy table and audit ledger |\n| `issuer` | OpenID Connect token issuer URL | Strict HTTPS URL (1 to 500 characters) | Required | Validates incoming JWT `iss` claim; plaintext HTTP rejected |\n| `client_id` | Expected audience claim | String (1 to 255 characters) | Required | Matches incoming JWT `aud` claim |\n| `subject_pattern` | Subject matching pattern | Glob or Regex (1 to 500 characters) | Required | Matches incoming JWT `sub` claim (e.g. `repo:org/repo:*`) |\n| `required_claims_json` | Exact claim match constraints | JSON Object | `{}` | Enforces additional claims (e.g. `{\"environment\": \"production\"}`) |\n| `surface` | Target MCP surface | `agent` or `docs` | `agent` | Determines which MCP endpoint the federated token can invoke |\n| `capability_mask` | Capability bitmask ceiling | Integer between 0 and 255 | 255 | Monotonic capability ceiling enforced by RLS |\n| `max_ttl_seconds` | Lifespan of issued ephemeral token | Integer between 60 and 86400 seconds | 3600 (1 hour) | Limits exposure window of federated workload credentials |\n| `is_enabled` | Policy active toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `true` | Disabled policies immediately reject token exchange |\n\n- **Built-in Presets:** The Studio provides one-click presets for common automated environments:\n  - **GitHub Actions:** Pre-fills issuer `https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com` and pattern `repo:org/repo:ref:refs/heads/main`.\n  - **GitLab CI:** Pre-fills issuer `https://gitlab.com` and pattern `project_path:group/project:ref_type:branch:ref:main`.\n  - **Kubernetes:** Pre-fills in-cluster issuer `https://kubernetes.default.svc.cluster.local` and pattern `system:serviceaccount:default:runner`.\n\n---\n\n## 7. OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio\nThe OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio (`/admin/oauth`) registers client applications, enforces PKCE code challenges, configures token lifetimes, and manages client revocation.\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Bulk Purge](images/slice3-oauth-governance-bulk-purge.png)\n\n\n### OAuth 2.1 Governance Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `client_name` | Name of the integrating client application | String (1 to 128 characters) | Required | Displayed on user authorization consent screens |\n| `client_id` | Unique OAuth client identifier | String prefixed with `kb_client_` | Auto-generated | Public application key used during authorization code flow |\n| `client_type` | Client confidentiality tier | `public`, `confidential` | `public` | Confidential clients require client secret authentication |\n| `auth_method` | Client authentication method | `none`, `client_secret_post`, `client_secret_basic` | `none` for public | Governs how client verifies identity at token endpoint |\n| `redirect_uris` | Allowed callback URLs for authorization codes | List of strict HTTPS URLs (localhost allowed for dev) | Required | Exact string matching enforced; wildcards strictly forbidden |\n| `pkce_enforced` | Proof Key for Code Exchange requirement | Mandatory `S256` challenge | `S256` | Rejects plaintext code challenges; mitigates authorization code interception |\n| `allowed_scopes` | List of privilege scopes client can request | `agent`, `docs`, `admin`, `admin:read`, `admin:write`, `admin:destructive`, `read:issues`, `write:issues` | `agent` | Token capability bitmask cannot exceed granted scopes |\n| `access_token_ttl` | Lifetime of minted access tokens | Integer seconds (300 to 86400) | 3600 (1 hour) | Ephemeral lifetime limiting blast radius of token leakage |\n| `refresh_token_ttl` | Lifetime of rotating refresh tokens | Integer seconds (86400 to 2592000) | 2592000 (30 days) | Refresh tokens rotate upon use; reused tokens revoke family |\n| `max_state_len` | Maximum length of OAuth state parameter | 4096 characters | 4096 | Supports large serialized state payloads required by Google Gemini |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for client lifecycle view | `all`, `active`, `suspended`, `revoked` | `all` | Isolates active integrations from suspended or revoked clients |\n\n![OAuth Client Governance Studio Paginated 500 Clients](images/slice3-oauth-governance-paginated-500.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`client_name`:** Human-readable title of the client application (1 to 128 characters). Displayed prominently to users on authorization consent screens and within the administrative governance directory.\n- **`client_id`:** Unique identifier generated upon client registration, prefixed with `kb_client_`. Serves as the public identifier across OAuth 2.1 authorization and device authorization flows.\n- **`client_type`:** Distinguishes between `public` clients (single-page applications, native desktop tools like Cursor or Claude Desktop, and CLI binaries that cannot safeguard secrets) and `confidential` clients (backend server applications capable of storing secrets securely).\n- **`auth_method`:** Defines the client authentication mechanism used at `/oauth/token`. Public clients use `none` with mandatory PKCE `S256`. Confidential clients use `client_secret_post` (secret in POST body) or `client_secret_basic` (HTTP Basic authentication header).\n- **`redirect_uris`:** Exact list of authorized redirect callback URIs. Every entry must be a valid HTTPS URL, with `http://localhost` and `http://127.0.0.1` permitted exclusively for local development and desktop tools. Wildcards and open redirect patterns are strictly rejected.\n- **`pkce_enforced`:** Enforces Proof Key for Code Exchange (RFC 7636). \ud83d\udc4dkanban requires `code_challenge_method = S256` for all authorization code flows. Plain code challenges (`code_challenge_method = plain`) are rejected with `400 invalid_request`.\n- **`allowed_scopes`:** Space- or comma-delimited list of privilege scopes available to the client: `agent` (general execution), `docs` (documentation), `admin` (administrative operations), `admin:read` (read-only diagnostics), `admin:write` (configuration updates), `admin:destructive` (deletion tools), `read:issues`, and `write:issues`.\n- **`access_token_ttl`:** Lifetime in seconds of minted access tokens (configurable from 300 to 86,400 seconds, default 3,600 seconds / 1 hour). Minimizes the window of exposure if a bearer token is intercepted.\n- **`refresh_token_ttl`:** Lifetime in seconds of refresh tokens (86,400 to 2,592,000 seconds, default 30 days). Refresh tokens rotate upon every exchange; if a previously used refresh token is presented, the entire token family is revoked to prevent replay attacks.\n- **`max_state_len`:** Defines the maximum permitted length of the OAuth `state` parameter (4,096 characters). Specifically engineered to accommodate complex serialized routing and nonce payloads generated by Google Gemini and enterprise identity gateways. Requests exceeding 4,096 characters are rejected with HTTP 400.\n- **`status_filter`:** Filters displayed clients in the Governance Studio across four lifecycle states: `all` (complete registry), `active` (operational clients), `suspended` (temporarily blocked from token exchange), and `revoked` (permanently disabled).\n\n![Device Activation Page](images/slice3-device-activate-page.png)\n\n### RFC 8628 `/activate` Device Flow and Bulk Purge\n\n- **RFC 8628 `/activate` Device Authorization Flow:** For terminal agents and CLI tools unable to host local redirect servers, \ud83d\udc4dkanban implements RFC 8628 device authorization:\n  - **Initiation:** The CLI calls `POST /oauth/device/authorize` with `client_id` and target `resource` (e.g. `https://kanban.example.com/mcp/agent`).\n  - **User Code Formatting:** The server issues an 8-character `user_code` formatted in hyphenated Base20 (`XXXX-XXXX`) using charset `BCDFGHJKLMNPQRSTVWXZ` (excluding vowels `A,E,I,O,U` to avoid offensive words and numbers `0-9` to eliminate visual ambiguity).\n  - **Activation Interface:** The developer visits `/activate`, enters the code, reviews the requested resource and scopes, selects permitted projects, satisfies AAL2 step-up if administrative scopes are requested, and approves the session.\n  - **Token Exchange:** The CLI polls `POST /oauth/token` with `grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code` at the configured interval (default 5s) until approval completes, receiving an ephemeral scoped MCP access token.\n- **Single-Transaction Bulk DCR Client Purge:** High-volume test suites and dynamic client registration can create hundreds of orphaned client rows. The Governance Studio includes a **Purge Stale Clients** action (`POST /api/v1/admin/oauth-clients/bulk-delete`). The backend identifies all public DCR clients with zero active grants and zero refresh tokens, deleting up to thousands of stale records in a single atomic database transaction while preserving active production integrations intact.\n\n---\n\n## 8. AI Providers Studio\nThe AI Providers Studio (`/admin/ai`) registers model providers, configures write-only API keys, and sets monthly token consumption caps.\n\n\n### AI Providers Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `provider_key` | Underlying AI provider implementation | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama`, `custom` | `gemini` | Selects prompt serializer, tool calling dialect, and streaming pipeline |\n| `priority` | Fallback dispatch order when multiple providers are active | Integer (1 to 100, higher runs first) | 10 | Primary provider attempted first; falls back on rate limit or 5xx |\n| `api_endpoint` | Base URL for API communications | Valid HTTP/HTTPS URL | Official cloud endpoint | Local Ollama uses `http://localhost:11434`; cloud requires HTTPS |\n| `api_key` | Cryptographic API secret for provider access | String (Write-only custody) | None | Stored encrypted in PostgreSQL; never sent back to browser client |\n| `monthly_token_cap` | Monthly token consumption budget ceiling | Non-negative integer (0 = unlimited) | 1,000,000 | Blocks cloud invocations once budget is reached to prevent cost overruns |\n| `default_model` | Model identifier used for sparkle and transition gates | String (e.g. `gemini-2.5-flash`, `gpt-4o`) | Provider default | Balances reasoning capability against response latency |\n| `temperature` | Randomness parameter for model generations | Float between 0.0 and 1.0 | 0.2 | Low temperatures ensure deterministic responses for code and QA gates |\n\n---\n\n## 9. Agent Definitions Studio\nThe Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`) authors system personas, binds autonomous agents to service accounts, and configures execution harnesses.\n\n\n### Agent Definitions Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `agent_name` | Unique name and identifier for the autonomous agent | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Used in ticket mentions, activity streams, and assignment cards |\n| `system_prompt` | Core instructions, persona guidelines, and security boundaries | UTF-8 Markdown text | Required | Directs model reasoning; stripped of dangerous injection sequences |\n| `execution_harness` | Runtime environment executing agent invocations | `api_direct`, `cli_pipe`, `remote_runner` | `api_direct` | `cli_pipe` uses zero-shell `agy -p` or `codex -p` in Linux jail |\n| `cli_pipe_binary` | Binary path used for zero-shell CLI pipe harness | Pre-approved binary name (`agy`, `codex`) | `agy` | Executes without shell or bash; communicates strictly over STDIN/STDOUT |\n| `service_principal_id` | Bound service account identity for database RLS | Valid UUID of service principal | Auto-provisioned | Binds database permissions to caller identity via `SET LOCAL` |\n| `repository_access` | Git repository access mode granted to the agent | `none`, `ro_mount`, `ephemeral_clone` | `none` | `ro_mount` mounts repository strictly read-only for forensic QA |\n\n---\n\n## 10. Remote Runners Studio\nThe Remote Runners Studio (`/admin/agent-runners`) configures distributed build nodes, mints enrollment tokens, and monitors mTLS heartbeats over dedicated port `:8444`.\n\n\n### Remote Runners Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `runner_name` | Unique hostname or identifier of the runner node | String (1 to 64 characters) | Required | Displayed in runner health dashboard and active job telemetry |\n| `enrollment_token` | Single-use cryptographic token for runner registration | Base64-encoded HMAC string | Minted on demand | Expires after 15 minutes (900 seconds max); grants certificate signing request (CSR) |\n| `mtls_cert_fingerprint`| SHA-256 fingerprint of validated client certificate | Hexadecimal SHA-256 hash | Registered upon enroll | Hardware-bound identity verifying mutual TLS WebSocket connection over port :8444 |\n| `runner_cli_command` | Standard enrollment invocation command | Shell command string | Generated on mint | Syntax: `echo \"$TOKEN\" \\| uv run kanban-agent-runner enroll --server-url <origin> --token-stdin --pool <pool>` |\n| `max_concurrent_jobs` | Maximum simultaneous task executions allowed on runner | Integer between 1 and 16 | 2 | Throttles runner workload to prevent resource exhaustion |\n| `heartbeat_timeout_s` | Maximum allowable duration without ping before offline | Integer between 10 and 120 seconds | 30 | Dead runners are marked offline and active jobs rescheduled |\n| `allowed_repo_remotes` | Whitelist of Git repository URLs runner can clone | Comma-separated list of repository URLs | Empty (All authorized) | Prevents runner exploitation for arbitrary network exfiltration |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Runner Certificate Rotation Lifecycle:** Runner client certificates are hardware-bound and cannot be silently renewed. To rotate a certificate, revoke the runner in `/admin/agent-runners` (AAL2 challenge confirmed), mint a fresh enrollment token (15-minute TTL), and re-enroll the runner daemon.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Standalone & Promotion Studio\nThe Standalone & Promotion Studio (`/admin/standalone`) inspects deployment topologies, triggers promotion to multi-container compose, and exports encrypted backups.\n\n![Standalone Status Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-standalone-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Standalone & Promotion Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `deployment_mode` | Current platform runtime environment | `standalone_embedded_pg`, `production_multi_container` | Detected at boot | Gates availability of nsjail container sandboxes and Redis hubs |\n| `passphrase` | Secret passphrase used to derive AES-256 backup key | String (Minimum 16 characters) | User-supplied | Encrypts archive into streaming KBKP or snapshot KBBAK1 format |\n| `auto_backup_enabled` | Recurring automated background snapshot toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Instructs .kb_ops.sock daemon to create automated snapshots |\n| `backup_interval_hours`| Frequency of automated snapshot execution | Integer between 1 and 168 hours | `24` | Governs Recovery Point Objective (RPO) and background IO scheduling |\n| `snapshot_retention_count` | Number of historical snapshot archives retained on server disk | Integer between 1 and 90 | `7` | Purges older snapshots automatically while keeping latest N snapshots |\n| `confirm_overwrite` | Confirmation phrase required for destructive restore | Exact string `\"RESTORE\"` | None | Prevents accidental database erasure during snapshot restoration |\n| `backup_storage_path` | Local filesystem path where backups are stored | Directory path (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`) | `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` | Isolated storage managed by `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon |\n| `compose_networks` | Network isolation topology for exported compose bundle | 5 networks (`edge_net`, `app_net`, `data_net`, `sandbox_net`, `egress_llm_net`) | 5 networks | Eliminates single-network lateral movement across services |\n\n![Standalone Backups and Snapshot History](images/slice4-standalone-backups-snapshot-history.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`deployment_mode`:** Detects whether \ud83d\udc4dkanban is executing inside a single standalone container (`standalone_embedded_pg`, typically launched via `docker run -p 8080:8080 thumbs-up-kanban:latest` with internal PostgreSQL 16) or within a compartmentalized production cluster (`production_multi_container`). In standalone mode, external Redis is optional and background tasks run in-process, whereas multi-container mode unlocks dedicated worker containers and nsjail container isolation.\n- **`passphrase`:** The client-supplied cryptographic passphrase submitted during manual streaming backup download (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/backup`) and database restoration (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). Must be at least 16 characters in length. The server derives an AES-256-GCM symmetric key via PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 across 600,000 iterations for KBKP streaming downloads, or Argon2id for KBBAK1 archives.\n- **`auto_backup_enabled`:** Toggles automated recurring background snapshots executed by the root-privileged `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon. When enabled, the daemon captures full PostgreSQL state and object graph assets into `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` using the timestamped `KBBAK1` format without interrupting active HTTP or WebSocket connections.\n- **`backup_interval_hours`:** Configures the interval between automated snapshot runs. The backend permits values between 1 and 168 hours (default 24 hours). This setting determines how frequently the `/run/postgresql/.kb_ops.sock` daemon wakes to execute scheduled pg_dump snapshot routines.\n- **`snapshot_retention_count`:** Defines the maximum number of automated snapshot `.kbbak` archives preserved within `/var/lib/kanban/data/backups` (configurable from 1 to 90, default 7). When a newly generated snapshot exceeds this threshold, the snapshot manager unlinks the oldest archives while verifying that at least the configured retention count remains accessible.\n- **`confirm_overwrite`:** The mandatory safety gate required by the destructive database restore endpoint (`POST /api/v1/admin/standalone/restore`). The payload must contain the exact uppercase string `\"RESTORE\"`. Any request containing mismatched, lowercase, or omitted confirmation tokens is immediately rejected with HTTP 422 before any database sequence is touched or tables are dropped.\n- **`backup_storage_path`:** The server-side directory where automated `.kbbak` archives reside (`/var/lib/kanban/data/backups`). This path is protected with restrictive permissions and managed exclusively by the fixed-operation socket daemon.\n- **`compose_networks`:** The 5-network topology exported by `GET /api/v1/admin/standalone/compose-export`. Separates traffic across `edge_net` (Caddy TLS ingress), `app_net` (FastAPI backend), `data_net` (PostgreSQL 16 and Redis), `sandbox_net` (untrusted Wasm and runner execution), and `egress_llm_net` (outbound AI provider traffic). Accompanied by a secret-scrubbed `.env.example` file.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Migration Import Studio\nThe Migration Import Studio (`/admin/imports`) provides lossless data ingestion from Plane, Jira, GitHub Issues, Linear, and canonical portable workspace bundles.\n\n![Migration Import Studio Multi-Source Tabs](images/slice4-imports-multi-source-tabs.png)\n\n\n### Migration Import Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `workspace_slug` | Destination workspace tenant identifier | Lowercase alphanumeric ASCII with hyphens | Active workspace | Attaches imported entities to specified tenant under PostgreSQL RLS |\n| `project_key` | Destination project prefix key (e.g. `KAN`, `JIRA`) | Uppercase ASCII alphanumeric (2 to 10 chars) | Bundle default | Re-keys issues sequentially while preserving historical keys as aliases |\n| `dry_run` | Non-destructive simulation toggle | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Calculates entity diffs inside a database savepoint without persisting |\n| `user_map` | Mapping dictionary from source users to canonical users | JSON dictionary of strings | `{}` | Binds historical authors, assignees, and commenters to active principals |\n| `export_workspace_slug` | Target workspace for generating portable bundles | Existing workspace slug | Current | Exports canonical `.tar.gz` archive containing entities, docs, and links |\n| `export_scrub` | Redacts sensitive secrets and tokens during export | Boolean (`true` / `false`) | `false` | Scrubs passwords, bearer tokens, and credentials from markdown before export |\n\n![Migration Import Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-imports-1440x900-light.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`per_source_endpoints`:** Ingestion is executed via 4 distinct per-platform endpoints: `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/plane` (Plane workspace JSON), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/jira` (Jira Cloud/Server JSON or REST dump with ADF support), `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/github` (GitHub Issues REST/CLI JSON), and `POST /api/v1/admin/imports/linear` (Linear JSON archive). Each parser normalizes rich text into sanitized UTF-8 CommonMark while strictly rejecting Unicode lone surrogates.\n- **`workspace_slug` and `project_key`:** Defines the destination tenant context and issue key prefix for the imported dataset. If specified, issues are allocated sequential integer sequence IDs under the destination project key (e.g. `KAN-1`, `KAN-2`). If left empty, the importer provisions or resolves the workspace and project names specified within the input bundle.\n- **`dry_run`:** Controls whether database modifications are committed. When set to `true`, the importer runs within a PostgreSQL `SAVEPOINT`, parses all entities, validates foreign keys, checks user mappings, and emits an `ImportDiffReport` before rolling back the savepoint. When set to `false`, the operation executes within an atomic database transaction that commits only upon 100% validation success.\n- **`user_map`:** A JSON dictionary mapping source account identifiers, legacy usernames, or email addresses to canonical \ud83d\udc4dkanban user accounts (e.g. `{\"legacy_dev\": \"alice@example.com\"}`). Unmapped accounts fall back to system defaults, ensuring that ownership, assignments, and historical comments remain traceable.\n- **`legacy_key_aliases`:** Upstream issue keys (such as `JIRA-101`, `GH-88`, `ENG-42`) are automatically extracted and recorded in the `issue_key_aliases` catalog. When queries arrive for obsolete keys (`GET /api/v1/issues/{issue_key}`), API clients receive `200 OK` JSON with header `X-Kanban-Canonical-Key: {canonical_key}`, while browser requests requesting `text/html` receive `HTTP 308 Permanent Redirect` with `Location: /projects/{project_key}/issues/{canonical_key}`.\n- **`export_workspace_slug` and `export_scrub`:** Used by the portable workspace exporter (`POST /api/v1/admin/exports/portable`) to generate a compressed `.tar.gz` bundle. When `scrub=true` (`export_scrub` in UI), secret patterns and authentication tokens are actively redacted from prose fields before packaging.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Admin Diagnostics & Realtime Probes Studio\nThe Admin Diagnostics Studio (`/admin/diagnostics`) monitors subsystem health, executes active telemetry probes, and tracks container resource limits.\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-diagnostics-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Admin Diagnostics Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `postgresql_rls` | PostgreSQL connectivity, pool saturation, migrations, RLS | 82 migrations, 100 tables `FORCE RLS` | Continuous | Core persistence and tenant isolation; latency threshold < 15ms |\n| `realtime_hub_outbox` | Transactional outbox relay and dispatch loop | Backlog depth, lag seconds, listener status | Continuous | Event delivery to WebSocket subscribers; lag threshold < 5s |\n| `job_worker_pool` | Background asynchronous task execution engine | Active tasks, worker concurrency, DLQ depth | Continuous | Processes asynchronous jobs; dead-letter queue must equal 0 |\n| `wasm_script_sandbox` | Wasmtime AST compiler and script sandbox execution | Compiler state, memory limits, fuel budget | Continuous | Sandboxed custom fields; fuel caps prevent infinite execution loops |\n| `agent_runtime_providers`| Health and latency of active AI model providers | `openai`, `gemini`, `ollama` provider status | Continuous | Informs sparkle completions and AI gates; reports monthly budget |\n| `remote_runner_fleet` | Connected mTLS build runner nodes over port :8444 | Online runner count, heartbeat timestamp | Continuous | Distributed workload execution; heartbeats must arrive within 30s |\n| `rate_limiter` | In-memory token bucket and IP throttle health | Bucket capacity, blocked IP count, error rate | Continuous | Protects API endpoints from volumetric denial-of-service |\n| `realtime_pubsub_probe` | Active PostgreSQL LISTEN/NOTIFY latency measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 1.0ms) | On Demand | Validates database pub/sub bus responsiveness |\n| `websocket_ws_probe` | Real WebSocket ping/pong frame roundtrip measurement | Latency milliseconds (target < 50ms) | On Demand | Validates client-to-server WebSocket bidirectional messaging |\n\n![Admin Diagnostics Realtime Probe Active](images/slice4-diagnostics-realtime-probe-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`postgresql_rls`:** Evaluates database health via `GET /api/v1/admin/diagnostics`. Asserts that the single-head Alembic migration tree is fully applied (82/82 migrations), connection pool saturation remains under 75%, and all 100 application tables enforce `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`. Connection latency is sampled continuously with healthy thresholds < 15ms.\n- **`realtime_hub_outbox`:** Tracks the transactional outbox relay daemon (`database/outbox/relay.py`). Measures the unprocessed message queue depth, average dispatch lag (healthy < 5s), and PostgreSQL `LISTEN/NOTIFY` channel subscription status. Stalled outboxes trigger immediate administrative alerts.\n- **`job_worker_pool`:** Monitors asynchronous background task executors responsible for webhook dispatch, automated snapshot creation, and audit logging. Reports total active tasks, concurrency utilization, and dead-letter queue (DLQ) depth. Any non-zero DLQ count indicates unhandled job failures requiring inspection.\n- **`wasm_script_sandbox`:** Monitors the in-process Wasmtime runtime executing custom field formulas and transition helpers. Verifies compiler initialization, memory consumption caps (default 32 MiB / 33,554,432 bytes per instance), and fuel budget limits preventing CPU exhaustion.\n- **`agent_runtime_providers`:** Asserts the availability and response latency of registered AI model providers (`gemini`, `openai`, `ollama`). Reports monthly token consumption against configured budget ceilings, warning administrators before cloud budget caps halt automated transition gates.\n- **`remote_runner_fleet`:** Tracks external execution nodes connected via mutual TLS (mTLS) over dedicated port `:8444`. Reports active runner counts, certificate expiration dates, and heartbeat timestamps. Runners failing to report within 30 seconds are marked offline and their active leases rescheduled.\n- **`rate_limiter`:** Reports the runtime state of in-memory token bucket rate limiters across public and authenticated routes. Tracks blocked request volumes and active IP throttle mappings, ensuring system resilience against abusive traffic.\n- **`realtime_pubsub_probe` and `websocket_ws_probe`:** On-demand latency probes. The pub/sub probe (`POST /api/v1/admin/diagnostics/probes/realtime`) injects a synthetic outbox notification and captures `LISTEN/NOTIFY` roundtrip time (typically ~0.38ms). The WebSocket probe initiates a live `/ws` binary ping frame in the operator's browser, measuring connection setup (~13.7ms) and ping/pong roundtrip latency (~0.44ms).\n\n---\n\n## 14. Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio\nThe Universal Object Graph Link Auditor Studio (`/admin/link-auditor`) inspects, classifies, and remediates referential integrity anomalies across all registered entities.\n\n![Link Auditor Studio - Desktop Light](images/slice4-link-auditor-1440x900-light.png)\n\n\n### Link Auditor Studio Configuration Table\n\n| Field Name | What It Controls | Allowed Values / Format | Default | Security / Operational Impact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `audit_ids` | Flagged link audit record UUIDs selected for purge | List of 1 to 200 UUIDs | Mandatory in purge | Scope of destructive link deletion in single atomic transaction |\n| `reason` | Mandatory administrative justification for link purge | String (10 to 500 characters) | Mandatory in purge | Recorded in immutable activity ledger; rejects < 10 chars with 422 |\n| `status_filter` | Filter tabs for inspecting audit record lifecycle | `unpurged`, `purged`, `resolved`, `all` | `unpurged` | Isolates active anomalies from resolved historical records |\n| `reason_filter` | Filters links by formal invalidity category | All 7 `InvalidLinkReason` codes | `ALL` | Isolates specific defect patterns for forensic inspection |\n\n![Link Auditor Integrity Scan Active](images/slice4-link-auditor-integrity-scan-active.png)\n\n### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs\n\n- **`audit_scan`:** Triggered via `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:audit` (or the **Run Integrity Scan** button). Traverses `object_links` and returns summary counts across all 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` codes without acquiring table locks.\n- **`reason_filter`:** Allows administrators to filter detected anomalies across the 7 formal `InvalidLinkReason` classes:\n  1. `DANGLING_SOURCE`: Source object UUID does not exist in the universal object spine.\n  2. `DANGLING_TARGET`: Target object UUID does not exist in the database.\n  3. `SOFT_DELETED_ENDPOINT`: One or both endpoints reference an object soft-deleted beyond the retention window.\n  4. `ARCHIVED_POLICY_VIOLATION`: Link connects active and archived entities violating project archiving rules.\n  5. `TYPE_MATRIX_VIOLATION`: Relation violates the registered link type matrix allowlist.\n  6. `CROSS_WORKSPACE_LEAK`: Link connects entities across disjoint workspace boundaries without federation.\n  7. `PERMISSION_SEVERED`: Endpoints span boundaries where caller or entity permissions have been severed.\n- **`audit_ids`:** The explicit list of 1 to 200 audit record UUIDs submitted to `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. Submissions with fewer than 1 or more than 200 items are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`reason`:** The mandatory rationale string required by `POST /api/v1/admin/object-links:purge`. The string must contain at least 10 non-whitespace characters (e.g. `\"Decommissioning orphaned test links post-cutover\"`). Rationale strings containing fewer than 10 characters are rejected with HTTP 422.\n- **`status_filter`:** Selects the display subset in the Studio UI: `unpurged` (flagged items requiring operator intervention), `purged` (historically purged records), `resolved` (links where the underlying anomaly was repaired), or `all`.\n- **`immutable_audit_ledger`:** Every completed purge operation writes an immutable `INVALID_OBJECT_LINK_PURGED` event to `activity_records`. The response returns `purged_count` and the list of `activity_ids` emitted to the ledger for forensic verification.\n"
